Two separate problems.

1. New topics still get one replica. kafka-topics.sh --create without --replication-factor creates the topic through the Admin API with the replication factor unset (-1). For that path Kafka always takes default.replication.factor from the controllers' configuration; the broker value matters only for auto-created topics. Your controllers still have the default, 1. Add default.replication.factor=3 to controller.properties on all three controllers and restart them one at a time (the setting is read-only). Keep it on the brokers, and pass --replication-factor 3 in the provisioning script anyway.

2. Existing topics. kafka-topics.sh --alter cannot change the replication factor, as the errors show. Do not delete and recreate invoices: that throws its data away. Use a reassignment:
   - write increase-rf.json with three replicas per partition, keeping the current replica first, e.g. {"topic":"invoices","partition":0,"replicas":[3,1,4]}, for all 12 partitions;
   - bin/kafka-reassign-partitions.sh --bootstrap-server kafka-1:9092 --reassignment-json-file increase-rf.json --execute --throttle 50000000, and keep the printed current assignment for rollback;
   - run the same command with --verify until every partition reports completed; --verify also removes the throttle;
   - check kafka-topics.sh --describe: ReplicationFactor: 3 and three brokers in Isr.
Then do the same for the other RF-1 topics, and consider min.insync.replicas=2 with acks=all for invoices.

alterConfigs is gone from Admin since 4.0; use incrementalAlterConfigs, which changes only the keys you name, so other overrides stay. Two more fixes: 'forever' is retention.ms=-1 (0 would delete data almost at once), and segment.ms is removed with a DELETE operation so the topic follows the server default.

```java
Map<ConfigResource, Collection<AlterConfigOp>> ops = new HashMap<>();
for (TopicRetention t : file.topics()) {
    ConfigResource res = new ConfigResource(ConfigResource.Type.TOPIC, t.name());
    String ms = t.forever() ? "-1" : Long.toString(t.retention().toMillis());
    List<AlterConfigOp> list = new ArrayList<>();
    list.add(new AlterConfigOp(new ConfigEntry("retention.ms", ms), AlterConfigOp.OpType.SET));
    if (t.dropSegmentMs()) {
        list.add(new AlterConfigOp(new ConfigEntry("segment.ms", ""), AlterConfigOp.OpType.DELETE));
    }
    ops.put(res, list);
}
AlterConfigsResult result = admin.incrementalAlterConfigs(ops);
for (Map.Entry<ConfigResource, KafkaFuture<Void>> e : result.values().entrySet()) {
    try {
        e.getValue().get();
        System.out.println(e.getKey().name() + ": ok");
    } catch (ExecutionException ex) {
        System.out.println(e.getKey().name() + ": failed: " + ex.getCause());
    }
}
```
(main declares throws InterruptedException.)

svc-retention needs AlterConfigs on the topics (an ACL on the topic names or a prefix), and DescribeConfigs if you add a check with describeConfigs afterwards.

The connector returns one task config per shard and ignores maxTasks: with 10 shards and tasksMax 6 it generates 10 configs, and by default (tasks.max.enforce) a connector that generates more tasks than tasks.max fails. It also reads the shard list once in start(), so new shards stay unknown until the connector restarts. Do not set tasks.max.enforce: false; it is deprecated and only hides the problem.

public void start(Map<String, String> props) {
  this.props = props;
  this.client = LedgerClient.connect(props.get("ledger.url"));
  this.shards = List.copyOf(client.listShards());
  monitor = Executors.newSingleThreadScheduledExecutor();
  monitor.scheduleAtFixedRate(() -> {
    List<String> now = List.copyOf(client.listShards());
    if (!new HashSet<>(now).equals(new HashSet<>(shards))) {
      shards = now;
      context.requestTaskReconfiguration();
    }
  }, 60, 60, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
}

public List<Map<String, String>> taskConfigs(int maxTasks) {
  List<String> current = shards;
  int n = Math.min(maxTasks, current.size());
  List<Map<String, String>> configs = new ArrayList<>();
  for (int t = 0; t < n; t++) {
    List<String> mine = new ArrayList<>();
    for (int i = t; i < current.size(); i += n) mine.add(current.get(i));
    Map<String, String> c = new HashMap<>(props);
    c.put("task.shards", String.join(",", mine));
    configs.add(c);
  }
  return configs;
}

public void stop() { if (monitor != null) monitor.shutdownNow(); }

(shards is a volatile field.) Each task keeps reading its shards from task.shards in start(). With 10 shards and tasksMax 6 you get 6 tasks, four of them with two shards.

The current code counts events, not state. builder.stream() makes subscriptions a record stream, so every CDC record for a subscription id counts once more: a plan change adds 1 to the new plan and leaves the old one untouched. KGroupedStream#count ignores records with null values, so cancellations (tombstones) never subtract.

Read the topic as a table and re-group the table:

StreamsBuilder builder = new StreamsBuilder();
KTable<String, Subscription> subs = builder.table("subscriptions",
    Consumed.with(Serdes.String(), subscriptionSerde),
    Materialized.as("subscriptions-store"));
KTable<String, Long> perPlan = subs
    .groupBy((id, sub) -> KeyValue.pair(sub.plan(), sub),
             Grouped.with("by-plan", Serdes.String(), subscriptionSerde))
    .count(Materialized.as("plan-counts-store"));
perPlan.toStream().to("plan-counts", Produced.with(Serdes.String(), Serdes.Long()));

Why it works: builder.table() treats a record for an existing id as an update and a null value as a delete. KTable#groupBy gives a KGroupedTable; its count() (like an aggregate with adder and subtractor) subtracts the old value from the old plan before adding the new value to the new plan, and for a tombstone calls only the subtractor. A move from basic to pro therefore gives basic -1 and pro +1, and a cancellation gives -1.

The dashboard should read plan-counts as a table (latest value per plan). Expect fewer, coalesced updates with the record cache on.

The draft rewinds on every assignment, so every restart and rebalance re-reads 3 days. Let committed offsets do their job and use the 4.x reset policy for the first start:

Properties p = new Properties();
p.put(ConsumerConfig.BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS_CONFIG, bootstrap);
p.put(ConsumerConfig.GROUP_ID_CONFIG, "refund-auditor");          // must be a group with no commits yet
p.put(ConsumerConfig.AUTO_OFFSET_RESET_CONFIG, "by_duration:PT6H");
p.put(ConsumerConfig.ENABLE_AUTO_COMMIT_CONFIG, false);
p.put(ConsumerConfig.ISOLATION_LEVEL_CONFIG, "read_committed");
p.put(ConsumerConfig.KEY_DESERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, StringDeserializer.class);
p.put(ConsumerConfig.VALUE_DESERIALIZER_CLASS_CONFIG, PaymentDeserializer.class);
try (KafkaConsumer<String, Payment> c = new KafkaConsumer<>(p)) {
  c.subscribe(List.of("payments"));
  while (running) {
    ConsumerRecords<String, Payment> recs = c.poll(Duration.ofMillis(500));
    for (ConsumerRecord<String, Payment> r : recs) audit.write(r.value());
    if (!recs.isEmpty()) c.commitSync();
  }
}

Why: auto.offset.reset is used only when the group has no committed offset for a partition (or it no longer exists). by_duration:PT6H (ISO-8601, positive) starts 6 hours before now on the very first start; afterwards the committed offsets win. Committing after audit.write, with auto commit off, gives at-least-once: a crash re-delivers, never skips. read_committed hides records of aborted transactions; the default read_uncommitted returns them. Use a new group.id; an old one would resume from its old commits.

Use a KTable-KTable foreign-key join, not the GlobalKTable lookup.

KTable<String, Shipment> shipments = builder.table("shipments", ...);
KTable<String, Account> accounts = builder.table("carrier-accounts", ...);
KTable<String, ShipmentView> view = shipments.join(accounts, s -> s.carrierAccountId(), ShipmentView::new);
view.toStream().to("shipment-view", ...);

Why: in a foreign-key join, an update of one account record produces an update for every shipment that references that account, which is exactly the requirement. A KStream-KTable or KStream-GlobalKTable join is triggered only by stream-side records; an account update only changes the join state, so open shipments would keep the old SLA until each shipment changes.

The partition counts are no obstacle: foreign-key joins do not require co-partitioning; Kafka Streams handles it with internal topics. (Shipments with a null carrierAccountId are ignored by the join; use leftJoin or a sentinel if they must appear.)

Cost: one SLA change on the largest account emits about 1.8 million updates x 1.5 KB = about 2.7 GB to shipment-view (times the replication factor on disk) in one burst, plus the internal subscription traffic. The record cache does not coalesce these, as every shipment key is different.

Missing numbers: how often large accounts change their SLA, and how many updates per second tracking-api can take. With those, decide whether the burst is acceptable or whether tracking-api should instead join the SLA at read time.

Two bugs: the KafkaConsumer is not thread-safe, so listTopics() from the HTTP thread throws ConcurrentModificationException; and liveness depends on the brokers, so a broker roll restarts all pods. Liveness should only say whether this process works; broker checks belong in readiness, if anywhere.

Track poll progress on the poll thread and only read it in the handler:

final AtomicLong lastPoll = new AtomicLong(System.currentTimeMillis());
final long maxPollIntervalMs = 300_000; // max.poll.interval.ms default

// poll thread
while (running) {
  ConsumerRecords<String, String> recs = consumer.poll(Duration.ofSeconds(1));
  lastPoll.set(System.currentTimeMillis());
  process(recs);
}

// HTTP thread: never touches the consumer
public void handle(HttpExchange ex) throws IOException {
  long age = System.currentTimeMillis() - lastPoll.get();
  int code = age < maxPollIntervalMs ? 200 : 503;
  ex.sendResponseHeaders(code, -1);
  ex.close();
}

If poll() is not called within max.poll.interval.ms the consumer is considered failed and the group rebalances anyway, so restarting a pod stuck that long is right; a broker outage does not stop poll() returning.

Probes: timeoutSeconds is unset, so every check times out after 1 s; set it, e.g. timeoutSeconds: 3. For the warm-up add a startupProbe on /healthz (periodSeconds: 10, failureThreshold: 15) so liveness starts only after startup succeeds; start lastPoll when warm-up ends.
